### Page 6 of 14

```text
UDC-24-168

October 7, 2024
1) The application is in accordance with the development policies and physical patterns
identified in the Comprehensive Plan.  (UDC 2.2.1.d.1)

PlanCheyenne encourages growth within the Urban Service Boundary and the maximization of
existing infrastructure. This property is within the Urban Service Boundary, in an area with
existing infrastructure.

PlanCheyenne encourages the conversion of homes along arterials and encourages
neighborhood revitalization. The applicant plans to redevelop this property with multiple uses
that would support the surrounding neighborhood.

The Future Land Use Designation for this property is Mixed-use Residential and the surrounding
area is largely Mixed-use Residential or Central Business District. To the northwest there is some
areas with the designation of Public. The Mixed-use Residential designation is primarily a mix of
residential uses. When near more urban and commercial areas the uses begin to mix and retail,
office and light trade are appropriate uses. These non-residential elements and other amenities
are important to enhance the neighborhoods of the surrounding area and provide additional
services.

It is also across the street from areas designated Central Business District. This designation
encompasses Downtown Cheyenne and functions as the community’s core business and civic
area. Many of the buildings and features in the district reflect the City’s historic roots, and
preservation efforts have retained many historic assets. Because the CBD is largely developed,
reinvestment and redevelopment are key areas of emphasis. This falls in line with what the
applicant is proposing as it is reinvestment into an existing property within an established area.

Based on the policies and future land use map, the UU Overlay is appropriate at this location.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 1.

2) The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering existing zoning,
the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies created under the guidance
of that plan for these areas; (UDC 2.2.1.d.2)

The proposed addition of the UU Overlay is compatible with the surrounding area. The UU
Overlay was written with this area of the community in mind and the whole area with the base
zone of MUR could be considered for the overlay (see review criterion #5). The comprehensive
plan supports infill development and redevelopment of sites. This UU Overlay would allow for
the property to redevelop. The property to the north received nearly identical consideration and
is zoned MUR, but was developed prior to the UU and had to receive relief to certain
requirements that the overlay would permit. The block the property is on is part of the DDA
district, demonstrating the appropriateness of applying regulations similar to those found in the
urban core.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 2.

3) The suitability of the subject property for development according to the standards and uses of
the existing zoning district, compared to the suitability of the subject property for
development according to the standards and uses of the proposed zoning district (UDC
2.2.1.d.3)
```

### Page 7 of 14

```text
UDC-24-168

October 7, 2024

The UU Overlay would allow there to be no parking minimum, similar to the CBD zone. It does
require bicycle parking to be provided and allows for the property to redevelop with commercial
uses much easier than the MUR would allow on its own. Conversion of a residential property to
commercial requires full compliance with the code, and would likely require multiple relief
processes to successfully redevelop in the MUR zone.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 3.

4) The ability of the subject property and surrounding vicinity to be developed according to the
Subdivision Standards in Article 4 considering the proposed zoning (UDC 2.2.1.d.4)

The zone change is occurring in a pre-existing neighborhood. No changes to the street network
are proposed. Accesses will not be changed with the zone change. Infrastructure (streets and
utilities) exist in the area.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 4.

5) The ability of the application to support the Intent and Applicability statements of the
proposed zoning district and achieve the Design Objectives of any specific standards. (UDC
2.2.1.d.5)

According to UDC 5.6.9.a and b, the UU overlay district is intended for Urban and Infill
Development. The UU district is intended to transition and step down from the urban core and
other urban areas of Cheyenne into the surrounding neighborhoods. The UU overlay district is
applicable to various lands envisioned for urban development. A base zone district of P, MUB, or
MUR must be in place in order for the UU overlay to apply.

This property has a base zone of MUR and is adjacent to the urban core and within the DDA
district. The downtown zone CBD exists across the street from the subject property. The
property is requesting UU for redevelopment in an established urban area of the community.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 5.

6) The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable standards for
development in the proposed zoning district, including other development options if any
specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after
approval. (UDC 2.2.1.d.6)

The UU Overlay does not change the base zone of the property, but adds additional standards or
reduces some of the requirements for the base zone. The overlay does make some specific uses
prohibited and allowed. The uses specifically prohibited by the UU are already prohibited in the
MUR, and the only newly-allowed use - Moderate Industrial – requires conditional use approval
according to the UU. Any new proposed development would be able to meet the requirements
of the overlay should the proposed development not happen.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 6.
```

### Page 8 of 14

```text
UDC-24-168

October 7, 2024
7) The review and recommendations of other reviewing bodies. (UDC 2.2.1.d.7)

Planning Staff would like to note the following as general comments:

1. Staff does not believe this to be spot zoning for the following reason:
a. The “classic” definition of spot zoning is “the process of singling out a small parcel of
land for a use classification totally different from that of the surrounding area for
the benefit of the owner of such property and to the detriment of other owners."
[Anderson’s American Law of Zoning, 4th Edition, § 5.12 (1995)]. The uses are
practically identical to the adjacent MUR, as it is still MUR (UU allows "Moderate
Industrial" as a conditional use without MUR allowing it, but otherwise it's identical)
b. The UU largely gives the land design standards which fall somewhere between
"CBD" and "MUR" design standards – and the property is on the border between
CBD and MUR.
c. The property immediately to the north received nearly identical consideration and is
zoned MUR, it was just developed before the UU existed (therefore they had to
receive relief from code requirements through alternative processes – instead of
getting the UU zone).

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 7.

8) Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general welfare. (UDC
2.2.1.d.8)

City staff is satisfied that this proposed zone change would not adversely affect the public
health, safety, or general welfare.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 8.

CONFORMANCE WITH APPLICABLE PLANS:

The project is located in an area covered by the following plans:
•
PlanCheyenne
Conformance with PlanCheyenne is covered by review criterion 1.

STAFF REVIEW:

Through the review process, review comments were received expressing the appropriateness of the
UU Overlay at this location.

CONCLUSION:

Staff is satisfied that the application meets the review criteria set forth in UDC 2.2.1.(d).  The UU Overlay
is appropriate based on the future land use map, surrounding zoning, surrounding development
patterns, and proposed development.
```

